  {
   "id": "sHsBKtMxJhIJ",
   "from": "Dennis Murphy",
   "author": "0c39e60ddb38622b",
   "date": "2010-10-24T12:20:35Z",
   "subject": "Re: Zero point on axis",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "HiL<br><br>Here&#39;s one way:<br><br># Toy example<br>d &lt;- data.frame(x = 1:10, y = 1:10)<br>ggplot(d, aes(x, y)) + geom_point() + xlim(0, 10) + ylim(0, 10) +<br>    expand_limits(x = 0, y = 0)<br><br>This makes the axis origin (0, 0). If you want to get rid of the padding so that the axes are flush with the L of the plot region, you need to add the following:<br>\n<br>    scale_x_continuous(expand = c(0, 0)) + scale_y_continuous(expand = c(0, 0))<br><br>The first value in expand is the multiplicative expansion factor, the proportion of &#39;padding&#39; between the edge of the plot region and the axis. The second value is an additive expansion parameter. This is mentioned in the help on scale_*_continuous(). The default is (0.05, 0), which means 5% padding around the perimeter and no additive expansion.<br>\n<br>Warning: If you do this, the points on the edges of the plot will be partially clipped, which is why it&#39;s usually a better idea to make the first coordinate in expand &gt; 0, perhaps 0.02 or more.<br><br>I always forget this, so as a reminder, expand is discussed in a paragraph on p. 99 of Hadley&#39;s book.<br>\n<br><br>HTH,<br>Dennis<br><br><br><div class=\"gmail_quote\"></div>"
    ],
    [
